      "source_body_markdown": "**A** A building can be a purely practical thing — four walls and a roof. Yet sometimes\nan architect turns those walls into something closer to a sculpture that can be walked\ninside. Three figures, working in very different times and places, show how far bold\ndesigners can stretch the idea of what a structure should look like.\n\n**B** Antoni Gaudí worked in Barcelona in the late nineteenth and early twentieth\ncenturies. He drew his flowing curves from plants, shells and skeletons, so that his\nbuildings look almost grown rather than built. His great church, the Sagrada Família,\nwith its spires rising like stone stalagmites, remains incomplete more than a century\nafter work began, and today's craftsmen still follow his designs.\n\n**C** Across the Atlantic, the American architect Frank Lloyd Wright pursued a very\ndifferent idea. Rather than reaching for the sky, many of his homes stretch low across\nthe landscape in long, level lines that seem to grow out of the ground. He called this\napproach organic architecture, believing a structure should blend with its setting\ninstead of imposing on it. His prairie houses, hugging the earth, are still among his\nbest-known work.\n\n**D** Closer to our own time, the British-Iraqi architect Zaha Hadid pushed form even\nfurther. Her buildings curve and flow in ways earlier builders could not have raised;\nit was only with new computer tools that such sweeping shapes became possible. In 2004\nshe became the first woman to win the field's most respected award, the Pritzker Prize,\nopening up a profession long dominated by men.\n\n**E** All three designers were mocked at some point. Gaudí's curves were called bizarre,\nWright's low roofs were thought strange, and Hadid's forms were dismissed by some as\nimpractical. It took time for the public to catch up with their vision.\n\n**F** What bound them was not a shared style — their styles could not be more different\n— but a willingness to treat a building as a question to be answered freshly, rather\nthan a pattern to be copied. Exactly how much each changed architecture is still\ndebated, but their influence is hard to overlook.",
