      "library": "l2_skill",
      "source_body_markdown": "**A** The story of flight is often shrunk to a single December morning in 1903, when a fragile machine lifted off a windy beach in North Carolina. Yet that morning rested on more than a century of patient work by people who never saw an aeroplane carry a passenger.\n\n**B** The first of them was the English engineer Sir George Cayley. Working in the early nineteenth century, long before any engine was light enough to fly, he set out the forces a flying machine must overcome — lift, weight, thrust and drag. He argued that lift, propulsion and control could be handled by separate parts of a craft, an idea that still underlies aircraft design today.\n\n**C** Decades later, the German engineer Otto Lilienthal turned theory into practice. Between 1891 and his death in a crash in 1896, he made around two thousand glider flights from hills near Berlin, recording careful data on how curved wings behaved. His measurements, and the photographs of him gliding, reached inventors across the Atlantic.\n\n**D** Among those inventors were the American brothers Wilbur and Orville Wright. Their decisive contribution was not the engine but control: they devised a system for steering an aircraft about three axes, so a pilot could keep it steady and turn it deliberately. Their first powered flights were short, low and watched by only a handful of people.\n\n**E** Public proof that powered flight had arrived came in Europe through the Brazilian Alberto Santos-Dumont. In 1906, before crowds in Paris, he flew his box-like 14-bis a few hundred metres — the first powered aeroplane flight there to be openly witnessed and officially recorded.\n\n**F** No single figure can claim to have invented flight. Each built on the failures and notebooks of others, and where exactly the credit should fall is still argued over, perhaps because the achievement was never really one person's to begin with.",
      "expected_metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "Cụm 'the first … to be + V3' nhấn mạnh tính 'đầu tiên/duy nhất' — chi tiết hay được hỏi trong matching và True/False (kiểm tra có đúng là 'đầu tiên' không).",
            "point": "Bị động dạng nguyên mẫu hoàn thành (perfect passive infinitive)",
            "review": "To-infinitive bị động: to be + V3. Sau danh từ có 'the first/only…' thường dùng to-infinitive bổ nghĩa (the first to do / to be done).",
            "example": "the first powered aeroplane flight there **to be openly witnessed and officially recorded**",
            "analysis": "'to be witnessed and recorded' là to-infinitive bị động (to be + V3), bổ nghĩa cho 'flight' theo nghĩa 'chuyến bay đầu tiên ĐƯỢC chứng kiến và ghi nhận'. Hai phân từ 'witnessed' và 'recorded' nối song song bằng 'and', cùng chia sẻ 'to be'."
          },
          {
            "tip": "Định vị danh từ đứng ngay trước 'that/which' để biết mệnh đề quan hệ đang mô tả cái gì — bước bắt buộc khi giải matching information.",
            "point": "Mệnh đề quan hệ xác định bổ nghĩa cho danh từ trừu tượng",
            "review": "Mệnh đề quan hệ xác định với 'that/which' cho vật, không dấu phẩy. Bị động với modal: modal + be + V3 (could be handled).",
            "example": "He argued that lift, propulsion and control could be handled by separate parts of a craft, **an idea that still underlies** aircraft design today",
            "analysis": "'an idea' là cụm đồng vị (appositive) tóm lại cả ý trước; 'that still underlies aircraft design today' là mệnh đề quan hệ xác định, 'that' thay 'an idea' và làm chủ ngữ của 'underlies'. Bên trong còn có 'could be handled by…' là bị động với modal."
          },
          {
            "tip": "Khi câu mở đầu bằng cụm chỉ nơi chốn rồi đến động từ 'to be/come', chủ ngữ nằm CUỐI câu — đọc ngược để xác định ai/cái gì là chủ ngữ.",
            "point": "Đảo ngữ với cụm giới từ chỉ nơi chốn đầu câu",
            "review": "Đảo ngữ với trạng ngữ chỉ nơi chốn/hướng đầu câu: Adverbial + V + S (Here comes…, Among them was…). Động từ chia theo chủ ngữ thật đứng sau.",
