    {
      "passage_id": "7ecb7e37-0a16-4b57-a123-7d10fafed24d",
      "slug": "matching-stages-of-language-learning",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "source_body_markdown": "**A** Few questions in the human sciences have proved as stubbornly contested as the one a child answers effortlessly: how does a young mind, in the space of a few short years, master a system of rules that even trained linguists struggle to describe? The twentieth century produced a sequence of rival answers, each illuminating and each, in time, found wanting in some respect.\n\n**B** The earliest of these to dominate was behaviourism, associated above all with B. F. Skinner. On this account, language is learned much as any other behaviour: through imitation and reinforcement. A toddler who says \"milk\" and is rewarded with both the drink and an approving smile is, in effect, being trained to repeat the utterance, while sounds that resemble adult speech are gradually shaped by the responses they provoke.\n\n**C** This tidy picture was famously dismantled by Noam Chomsky, who pointed out that children routinely produce sentences they cannot possibly have heard and been rewarded for. To explain such creativity, Chomsky proposed that the brain arrives already equipped with a \"universal grammar\" — an innate template, present from birth, that underlies the grammar of every human language and needs only exposure to a particular tongue to be set in motion.\n\n**D** Where the nativist account looks inward to biology, the social-interactionist tradition, traced to Lev Vygotsky, looks outward to relationships. For Vygotsky, thought and language alike take shape through dialogue with more capable partners — parents, teachers, older children — who guide the learner through tasks just beyond their independent reach. Language, on this view, is less a private endowment than a tool first borrowed from others and only later made one's own.\n\n**E** More recently, Michael Tomasello has advanced a usage-based approach that, in a sense, seeks middle ground. Rejecting the idea of a fixed inborn grammar, he argues that language emerges from more general cognitive gifts, above all the distinctively human capacity to read the shared intentions behind another person's actions. The child who grasps what an adult is trying to do is already halfway to grasping what the adult is trying to say.\n\n**F** No single theory has swept the field, and perhaps none should be expected to. It may be that each captures a genuine fragment of an extraordinarily complex process — that reinforcement, innate structure, social guidance and intention-reading all play their part, in proportions that remain difficult to fix. The debate, like the child's mind itself, resists any neat conclusion.",
      "expected_metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "'Few questions have proved as … as X' = X gây tranh cãi bậc nhất — cách nói nhấn mạnh thay cho 'X is the most contested'.",
            "point": "Cấu trúc so sánh 'as … as' + mệnh đề quan hệ rút gọn",
            "review": "as + adj + as = ngang bằng. 'Few + N' = nghĩa phủ định (ít đến mức gần như không). Mệnh đề quan hệ tân ngữ có thể lược 'that'.",
            "example": "Few questions in the human sciences **have proved as stubbornly contested as** the one a child answers effortlessly",
