      "source_body_markdown": "**A** A person who steps off a kerb, hears a horn and freezes will probably say, afterwards, that fear made the heart pound. Yet the ordering implied by that account — first the feeling, then the racing pulse — is precisely what a century of research on emotion has called into question. Where, in the sequence linking a stimulus, the body and the conscious mind, the emotion actually begins is a problem psychologists have answered in strikingly different ways.\n\n**B** The earliest systematic answer, advanced independently by William James and the Danish physician Carl Lange in the 1880s, inverted common sense. On their account, a frightening sight first triggers a bodily reaction — quickened breathing, a jolt of adrenaline, tensed muscles — and the conscious emotion is simply the mind's reading of those changes. We do not tremble because we are afraid, the slogan ran; we are afraid because we tremble. Each distinct emotion, in this picture, corresponds to its own distinct pattern of physiological response.\n\n**C** Walter Cannon, joined later by Philip Bard, found this implausible. He noted that fear, rage and even excitement are attended by much the same internal upheaval, so the body's response could hardly, by itself, distinguish one emotion from another. He proposed instead that a stimulus reaching the brain sends signals simultaneously down two routes — one producing the bodily arousal, the other producing the conscious feeling — so that the two arrive together rather than one causing the other.\n\n**D** A third position emerged in 1962, when Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer argued that arousal and interpretation must combine. In their experiment, volunteers received an injection of a stimulant and were then placed with an actor who behaved either cheerfully or angrily; those exposed to cheerfulness reported feeling happy, while those exposed to anger reported irritation, despite sharing an identical physiological state. Emotion, they concluded, requires two factors: a state of arousal and a cognitive label that the surrounding situation supplies.\n\n**E** More recent work has pushed the cognitive element earlier still. Richard Lazarus maintained that even the initial bodily reaction is shaped by appraisal — a rapid, often unconscious assessment of whether an event is threatening, welcome or irrelevant to one's goals. On this reading, the judgement does not merely name an arousal already underway but helps to set it off, so that two people facing the same event may respond quite differently from the very first instant.\n\n**F** No single account has wholly displaced the others, and contemporary theorists tend to treat them as describing different stages of one rapid process rather than rival explanations of the whole. Whether the felt emotion is best understood as the cause of the body's reaction, its consequence, or its inseparable partner remains a question on which the evidence, intriguingly, still points in more than one direction.",
