    {
      "id": "bc96875c-9ee2-5077-80e5-9979fe8854c1",
      "slug": "completion-how-a-microphone-works",
      "title": "Completion — How a Microphone Works",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "The microphone that captures a singer's voice or a podcaster's words performs a\ndeceptively simple task: it converts the energy of moving air into a pattern of\nelectricity. The idea dates back to the 1870s, when inventors such as Emile Berliner\nand David Edward Hughes built the first practical devices, but the physics behind the\nmost common modern design was actually described decades earlier, in Michael Faraday's\nwork on electromagnetic induction in 1831.\n\nAt the front of almost every microphone sits a thin, flexible sheet known as the\ndiaphragm. When a person speaks or sings, the resulting sound waves travel through the\nair and push against this sheet, making it vibrate in step with the pressure changes.\nThe diaphragm is light enough to respond to even quiet sounds, tracing out the shape of\nthe original noise in physical movement.\n\nIn a dynamic microphone — the rugged type used on most stages — the diaphragm is glued\nto a tiny coil of fine wire. This coil hangs inside the field of a permanent magnet. As\nthe diaphragm vibrates, it drives the coil back and forth through the magnetic field,\nand by the law of electromagnetic induction this motion generates a small electric\ncurrent that mirrors the sound. The signal is extremely weak, which is why every system\nneeds an amplifier to boost it before it can be recorded or broadcast.\n\nOther designs work differently; a condenser microphone, for instance, uses the\ndiaphragm as one plate of a charged capacitor rather than a coil and magnet. Yet the\nunderlying principle is shared. Whichever method is chosen, the chain runs the same way:\nair moves a surface, that movement is turned into a changing voltage, and the faint\ncurrent is then strengthened into a usable signal. How faithfully a microphone preserves\nthe finest detail of a sound, however, still depends on the quality of every link in\nthat chain.",
      "metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "Câu bị động đặt sự việc làm tâm điểm và lược/đẩy lùi tác nhân — khi điền completion, bám đối tượng (the physics, the signal) để tìm đúng từ khóa.",
            "point": "Câu bị động (passive voice) ở quá khứ",
            "review": "Bị động quá khứ: was/were + V3. Trạng từ thường đứng giữa be và V3 (was actually described).",
            "example": "the physics behind the most common modern design **was actually described** decades earlier",
            "analysis": "'was described' là bị động quá khứ đơn (was + V3); 'actually' chèn giữa trợ động từ và phân từ để nhấn mạnh sự bất ngờ. Chủ ngữ 'the physics' là đối tượng nhận hành động, tác nhân (Faraday) được nêu gián tiếp ở cụm sau."
