      "id": "08b28267-3a61-564f-a85e-4b0e74b359ae",
      "slug": "completion-how-cheese-is-made",
      "title": "Completion — How Cheese Is Made",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "Cheese is one of the oldest ways to stop milk from going bad, and people have been making\nit for more than 7,000 years. At its heart, cheese is simply milk that has been changed\ninto a thicker, more solid form. The milk used can come from cows, goats or sheep, and\nthis difference in milk is one reason why cheeses around the world taste so different.\n\nThe process begins when fresh milk is gently warmed in a large tank. Helpful bacteria are\nthen added, and these tiny living things slowly turn the milk sour. Next, the cheese-maker\nadds a substance called rennet. This makes the milk thicken and set, splitting it into\nsolid lumps called curds and a thin liquid called whey.\n\nOnce the curds have formed, they are cut into small pieces and stirred. The cheese-maker\nthen presses these solid lumps to push out the extra whey. The harder and longer the block\nis pressed, the firmer the final cheese will be. Salt is also often added at this stage,\nboth for flavour and to help the cheese keep for longer.\n\nFinally, the young cheese is left in a cool place to age, a process known as ageing. Many\ncheeses are stored for months, and some for years. During this time the flavour grows\nstronger and more complex. A simple block of cheese is therefore the result of time, care\nand a little everyday science.",
