      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "A modern wind turbine looks deceptively simple: three slim blades turning slowly at the\ntop of a tall tower. Behind that calm movement, however, lies a chain of steps that\nconverts the energy of moving air into electricity that homes can use.\n\nThe process begins with the blades themselves. Each blade is shaped like an aircraft\nwing, so that the air flowing past it creates lift rather than simply pushing it back.\nWhen the moving air pushes against these angled blades, they begin to turn, driving a\ncentral hub that rotates fairly slowly — often only a few dozen times a minute.\n\nThat slow speed is a problem, because a generator needs to spin much faster to work\nefficiently. To solve this, the slow shaft enters a gearbox. Inside, a set of gears\nraises the rotation speed sharply, turning the lazy spin into rapid rotation. This\nhigh-speed shaft then turns the generator, where the movement is turned into electric\ncurrent. The current runs down the inside of the tower and is sent onto the grid.\n\nThe wind does not always blow, though, and this is the technology's biggest limit. A\nturbine reaches full power only when the wind is strong and steady, so for much of the\nyear it produces far less than its top rating. If the wind grows too violent, the\nmachine must even shut itself down to avoid damage, feathering its blades so that they\nslip through the gusts instead of catching them. Engineers capture this uneven\nperformance through a figure called the capacity factor, which compares actual average\noutput with the theoretical maximum. Because no single machine can be relied on\ncompletely, turbines are usually built together in large groups known as wind farms,\nwhere their combined output is steadier than that of any single tower.",
      "metadata": {
