      "id": "1807c4eb-d117-5f8e-b592-2f23dee9b96c",
      "slug": "detail-emperor-penguins",
      "title": "Reading for Detail — Emperor Penguins",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "# Reading for Detail — Emperor Penguins\n\nHow would you like to live in the coldest place on Earth? For emperor penguins, this is normal life. These amazing birds live in Antarctica, where the weather is icy all year.\n\nThe emperor penguin is the tallest and heaviest penguin in the world. It can grow up to 120 centimetres tall and weigh as much as 40 kilograms. It has black and white feathers, with a soft yellow colour near its head.\n\nEmperor penguins are great swimmers. They can dive deeper than any other bird and can stay under the water for a long time while they hunt for fish.\n\nWinter in Antarctica is very hard. The mother penguin lays one egg, and then something special happens. The father holds the egg on his feet and keeps it warm while the mother goes to the sea to find food. He does not eat for many weeks.\n\nTo fight the cold, the penguins huddle together in a big group. The birds on the outside slowly move to the middle, so that everyone gets a turn to be warm.\n\nWhen the baby penguin is born, both parents work hard to feed it. Life is not easy on the ice, but the emperor penguin is well made for it.\n\n---\n\n*Practice tip: When you read for detail, find the exact words in the text that answer the question. Check that the facts really match.*",
