      "library": "l2_skill",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "# Reading for Detail — The Arctic Fox\n\nDo you think a small animal can live in one of the coldest places on Earth? The Arctic fox can do this. It lives in the cold lands of the far north, where the air can fall to minus 50 degrees.\n\nThe Arctic fox is well made for the cold. It has a thick, warm coat, short, small ears that help it keep heat in, and furry feet so the fox can walk on the cold ice.\n\nOne very special thing is that the fox's coat changes colour. In winter, the coat is white, so the fox is hard to see in the snow. In summer, when the snow melts, the coat turns brown to match the ground and rocks.\n\nFinding food in the north is not easy. The Arctic fox eats small animals, birds' eggs and berries. Sometimes it even follows a polar bear and eats the food the bear leaves behind. In these clever ways, the little fox stays alive in a world of ice.\n\n---\n\n*Practice tip: When you read for detail, find the exact words in the text that answer the question. Check that the facts really match.*",
