      "title": "How Plants Drink",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "Plants do not have a mouth. So how do they drink water? The answer is hidden under the\nground, below your feet.\n\nEvery **plant** has **roots**. The roots grow down under the **soil**. They take in water\nfrom the soil all day. Inside the plant, there are very small **tube**s. Water moves up\nthrough these thin tubes. It goes up from the roots to the **top** of the plant, even in\na very tall tree. Some trees are taller than 100 metres, but the water still\nreaches their highest leaves.\n\nBut what makes the water go up? Look at the **leaves**. Water can **evaporate** from the\nleaves into the air. When water evaporates, it **pull**s more water up. So the leaves\nhelp bring water to the top. This is a clever way to move water without a pump.\n\nA plant needs water to **grow** well and stay green. So next time you see a plant,\nremember it is quietly drinking all the time.",
