    {
      "id": "51cf4b09-1ee9-5fda-a7a4-66224fbec447",
      "slug": "how-we-taste-food",
      "title": "How We Taste Food",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "Your **tongue** has about 10,000 tiny **taste buds**. These tiny parts help you enjoy\nyour food. But how do we really **taste** things?\n\nTaste buds can find five main **flavours**. These are sweet, sour, salty, **bitter**,\nand a savoury taste called umami. Many people think the tip of the tongue only tastes\nsweet things. This old idea is **wrong**. Every part of the tongue can find every\nflavour.\n\nBut taste is only half the story. Your **nose** is also very important. Most of what\nwe call flavour really comes from **smell**. When you have a cold, your nose is\nblocked. Food can taste **bland** and boring.\n\nSo taste and smell work as a team. The next time you eat, breathe in slowly. You may\n**notice** more flavours than before. Eating is a job for both the tongue and the nose.",
