    {
      "id": "dc0739aa-bd02-5f05-8f92-a4062f1be3f4",
      "slug": "inference-ethics-of-de-extinction",
      "title": "Inference & Vocabulary in Context — The Ethics of De-extinction",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "In 1914, a passenger pigeon named Martha died in Cincinnati Zoo, and a species that had once darkened North American skies in flocks of billions was gone for good. A century later, the finality of that \"for good\" no longer feels certain. Advances in genetics have made it possible to imagine bringing vanished animals back, and with that possibility has come a thicket of ethical questions that the science itself cannot settle.\n\nThe leading projects are more modest than the word \"resurrection\" suggests. The company Colossal Biosciences, for instance, does not propose to clone a frozen mammoth; it aims to edit the genome of the Asian elephant, the mammoth's closest living relative, until the result carries key mammoth traits such as cold tolerance and shaggy hair. What would emerge is an approximation — an elephant engineered to resemble a mammoth — rather than the original creature reborn.\n\nOther efforts target more recently lost species. Australian researchers working on the so-called Lazarus Project managed to produce early embryos containing the genetic material of the gastric-brooding frog, an animal famous for raising its young in its stomach, which disappeared in the 1980s. Conservationists have likewise floated reviving the passenger pigeon by drawing on its nearest surviving cousin, the band-tailed pigeon.\n\nEven if such work succeeds technically, harder problems remain. A revived mammoth or pigeon would need somewhere to live, yet the grasslands and forests these animals once occupied have often been transformed or lost. Releasing a handful of engineered individuals into an altered ecosystem could disrupt the species that adapted in their absence, and a single specimen in a laboratory is hardly a restored species at all.\n\nCritics add a sharper worry: that the mere promise of reversal could weaken the case for protecting what still lives. If extinction looks undoable, the urgency of conservation may quietly drain away. Defenders reply that the techniques developed might also rescue endangered species before they vanish, and that the justification for the work lies as much in those tools as in any headline-grabbing mammoth. Where the balance finally falls is not yet clear, and the projects may prove valuable at least for the questions they force us to ask.",
      "metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "Khi câu mở đầu bằng cụm giới từ rồi đến động từ trước danh từ, chủ ngữ nằm CUỐI — đọc ngược để tìm chủ ngữ, tránh hiểu sai ai gây ra hành động.",
            "point": "Đảo ngữ với cụm giới từ chỉ phương hướng đầu câu (locative inversion)",
            "review": "Locative/adverbial inversion: cụm trạng ngữ đầu câu + V + S. Động từ chia theo chủ ngữ THẬT đứng sau (questions → has come... chủ ngữ tập hợp 'a thicket').",
            "example": "**with that possibility has come** a thicket of ethical questions that the science itself cannot settle",
            "analysis": "Cụm giới từ 'with that possibility' được đảo lên đầu, kéo theo đảo ngữ chủ–động: trợ động từ/động từ 'has come' đứng trước chủ ngữ thật 'a thicket of ethical questions…'. Chủ ngữ thật được bổ nghĩa bởi mệnh đề quan hệ 'that the science itself cannot settle' (that = tân ngữ, lược được). Đảo ngữ giúp giữ thông tin mới ở cuối câu."
          },
          {
