      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "When wolves were returned to Yellowstone National Park in 1995, after an absence of nearly seventy years, the effects rippled outward in ways few had predicted: elk grazed more warily, willows recovered along the riverbanks, and the rivers themselves are said to have shifted their course. That sequence has since become the founding parable of rewilding, a movement that asks not how nature might be managed more cleverly but whether, in certain places, it might be managed far less.\n\nRewilding resists tidy definition, yet at its heart lies a change of ambition. Conventional conservation tends towards stewardship — the patient tending of particular species or habitats towards some chosen target. Rewilding, by contrast, seeks to restore the broad processes that shape ecosystems — predation, grazing, flooding, decay — and then to withdraw, accepting whatever community of life those processes happen to produce. The term was given scientific grounding in 1998 by the conservation biologist Michael Soulé, often called the father of conservation biology, and his colleague Reed Noss. Its advocates speak of \"self-willed\" land, on which outcomes are not specified in advance and human control is deliberately surrendered.\n\nThe ecological case has become hard to dismiss. To reintroduce a vanished predator, or a lost engineer such as the beaver, can set off a trophic cascade — a chain of consequences that ripples down through an entire food web and may rebuild a richness that decades of careful management never achieved. Regrown wetlands and spreading scrub also lock away carbon, so that rewilding is now invoked, perhaps too readily, as a single answer to two crises at once.\n\nYet the ethical questions multiply the moment one asks what \"wild\" is supposed to mean. There is no neutral baseline waiting to be restored; every reference point we might choose quietly encodes a human preference dressed as nature. Animal welfare complicates things further. At Oostvaardersplassen in the Netherlands, a celebrated experiment in naturalistic grazing, the mass starvation of deer and horses through a series of hard winters forced an uncomfortable admission: letting nature take its course can look, to those watching, very like neglect. Rural communities, for their part, may experience a scheme presented as restoration as something closer to dispossession.\n\nNone of this amounts to an argument against rewilding so much as a caution against its more utopian framings. To return a measure of wildness to depleted land may rank among the more hopeful undertakings of the age; but to suppose that doing so lifts humanity out of the picture is to misread it entirely. Choosing where to step back, and how far, remains an unmistakably human act — and one whose consequences we shall be answerable for long after the wolves have settled in.",
      "metadata": {
