      "title": "Inference & Vocabulary in Context — The Paradox of Progress",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "By almost every objective measure, the inhabitants of wealthy nations live better than their great-grandparents did. They are richer, healthier and far less likely to die young; a child born today in such a country can expect decades of life that earlier generations were denied. Worldwide, the share of people in extreme poverty fell from about 36 per cent in 1990 to under 10 per cent by 2015, according to the World Bank. And yet survey after survey records no comparable rise in how satisfied people say they are with their lives. This stubborn gap between the ledger of material gain and the testimony of felt contentment is what economists and psychologists have come to call the paradox of progress.\n\nPart of the explanation lies in how the benefits of wealth are distributed across the range of incomes. Where people are poor, additional money buys relief from genuine hardship, and reported satisfaction climbs steeply with it. Once basic needs are comfortably met, however, the same increments of income purchase ever smaller gains in contentment, so that a doubling of national wealth may register as only a faint upward flicker on the happiness scale.\n\nA second factor is that human judgement is relentlessly comparative. People assess their circumstances not against some fixed standard but against those around them, so that a neighbour's rising income quietly becomes the benchmark by which a person rates their own. When everyone advances together, the relative position that so much effort secures scarcely changes, and the satisfaction expected from the advance fails to arrive — a treadmill on which running faster leaves one in much the same place.\n\nUnderlying both tendencies is a feature of temperament that researchers term adaptation. Windfalls and misfortunes alike tend to move mood only briefly before a characteristic resilience pulls it back towards a settled personal level; lottery winners and accident victims, famously, drift within a year or two towards much the same outlook they held before. If contentment is anchored in this way, no amount of accumulated progress will durably lift it.\n\nNone of this means that progress is hollow. The more cautious reading is that the paradox owes much to what the surveys actually capture, and that finer instruments — measures of meaning, health and momentary experience rather than a single global verdict — narrow the gap considerably. Whether the puzzle is a profound limit on the human condition or largely an artefact of crude measurement is a question the available evidence does not yet decisively settle.",
