      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "In 1945, as the Second World War ended, the philosopher Karl Popper set down a warning that has since travelled far beyond the book that contained it. A society committed to tolerance, he observed, harbours a hidden danger: if it tolerates without limit, including those who would dismantle tolerance itself, it may end by destroying the very openness it prizes. This is the so-called paradox of tolerance.\n\nPopper's reasoning is deceptively simple. Should the intolerant be granted unlimited freedom to act, and should the tolerant decline to defend themselves, then the tolerant are likely to be swept aside along with their principles. He therefore claimed, in the name of tolerance, the right not to tolerate the intolerant — the right to suppress, if necessary, movements bent on the destruction of free debate. The conclusion sounds paradoxical only until one sees that unconditional tolerance is, in effect, a standing invitation to its own undoing.\n\nWhat is too often forgotten is the restraint Popper attached to his own argument. He did not advise silencing intolerant doctrines wherever they appear; on the contrary, he held that as long as such views can be answered by reasoned argument and held in check by public opinion, suppression would be both unnecessary and unwise. The right to intervene arises, on his account, only when a movement forsakes argument altogether — when it answers words with fists and refuses, on principle, to meet its opponents in debate.\n\nThe principle, stated so cleanly, proves slippery in application. Who is to judge when persuasion has failed and a movement has crossed from advocacy into a genuine threat? The category of \"the intolerant\" is dangerously elastic, and a government armed with the right to suppress its enemies may be tempted to brand mere dissent as intolerance. Popper's paradox thus hands us a principle and, in the same breath, a permanent worry about its abuse.\n\nIt would be convenient if a clear rule could mark the point at which tolerance must, for its own sake, turn intolerant. No such rule has been found, and perhaps none can be. What Popper bequeathed is less a formula than a tension — a reminder that an open society must remain willing, in the last resort, to defend itself, while remaining honest about how readily that willingness can be misused. The line will always have to be drawn afresh, case by case, and argued over in public as the threats themselves change shape.",
