      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "On an ordinary afternoon, many people find that they have walked to the kitchen cupboard\nwithout quite remembering why. They did not decide to feel hungry; their feet seemed to\ncarry them there. These small, automatic moments, according to the journalist Charles\nDuhigg, are the signature of a habit at work, and understanding their structure may be\nthe first step to changing them.\n\nWhen an action is repeated often enough, the brain stops treating it as a fresh decision.\nInstead, the action shifts into a deeper, more automatic region that keeps running with\nalmost no conscious effort. This saves precious attention, but it also explains why\nhabits cling so stubbornly: they play out largely beyond the reach of willpower alone.\n\nResearchers often describe a habit as a loop with three linked parts. First comes a cue,\na trigger in the surroundings such as a time of day, a place or a particular mood. That\ncue sets off a routine, the behaviour itself. Finally comes a reward, a pleasant feeling\nthat tells the brain the loop is worth remembering and repeating next time.\n\nBecause the loop is so deeply set, trying to fight it head-on usually fails. A more\nrealistic strategy, Duhigg argues, is to keep the cue and the reward but replace the\nroutine in the middle. Someone who snacks when stressed, for example, might keep the\ncue of tension and the reward of relief, yet swap the snack for a short walk. By leaving\nthe familiar trigger and payoff untouched, the new routine has a better chance of taking\nhold.\n\nWhether such a change sticks depends on factors that science is still tracing, and no\nsingle trick works for everyone. Even so, seeing behaviour as a loop, rather than as a\nfailure of character, at least offers a clearer place to begin.",
