      "id": "a46384d4-d9a7-5465-8128-aa08bb1b76b3",
      "slug": "inference-the-psychology-of-colour-in-branding",
      "title": "Inference & Multiple Choice — The Psychology of Colour in Branding",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "When shoppers reach for a familiar product, the choice is often settled before a single\nword is read. A study by the marketing researcher Satyendra Singh, published in 2006,\nconcluded that people make up their minds about a product within ninety seconds, and\nthat as much as 90 per cent of that snap judgment can be based on colour alone. For the\ncompanies that design packaging, such findings turn the choice of a shade into a serious\ncommercial decision rather than a matter of taste.\n\nBrands use colour to do two jobs at once. The first is recognition. A consistent colour\nacts like a visual signature: the particular red of a soft-drink can or the pale blue of\na jewellery box can be identified across a crowded shelf long before the logo becomes\nclear. Singh's work suggested that roughly 80 per cent of consumers believe colour\nincreases their recognition of a brand, and some firms guard a signature shade as\nfiercely as a trademark.\n\nThe second job is to suggest a feeling. Banks and technology firms often favour blue,\nwhich is widely read as steady and trustworthy, while discount stores reach for red and\nyellow to hint at energy and low prices. Luxury brands, by contrast, tend to strip\ncolour away almost entirely, relying on black, white and generous empty space to imply\nrestraint and quality.\n\nNone of this works in isolation, however. The meaning a colour carries depends on\nculture, on the product it sits on and on what rival brands have already claimed. A\ngreen that signals health on a yoghurt pot may simply look cheap on a luxury watch.\nColour, in short, is a powerful but slippery tool: it can shape a first impression in an\ninstant, yet whether it can hold a customer's loyalty once the novelty fades remains far\nless certain.",
