      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "A quiet, polite person in the middle of a cheering crowd may end up shouting things\nthat he or she would never call out alone. This familiar observation has long pushed\nthinkers to ask whether a crowd truly changes the people inside it, or merely reveals\nwhat was already hidden within them.\n\nThe first to offer a famous answer was the French writer Gustave Le Bon, who in 1895\ndescribed a 'group mind' that takes over individuals, making them suggestible and\ncapable of acts they would avoid on their own. His vivid picture was hugely\ninfluential, but much of it owed as much to his fear of the mob as to careful\nobservation, and later researchers treated his strongest claims with caution.\n\nLater work suggested, however, that Le Bon had caught hold of something real. In\nexperiments where participants wore masks or identical uniforms, they often behaved\nmore harshly than when they could be clearly identified. When people felt hidden in the\ngroup, their everyday restraints seemed to loosen — an effect psychologists call\ndeindividuation. The sense of becoming hard to pick out, rather than the sheer number\nof people, was what mattered.\n\nA second factor is shared attention. A crowd is not merely many people in one place; it\nis many people whose attention and feeling are pulled the same way at the same time.\nWhen that common focus is strong — at a concert, a match, or a protest — individuals\ntend to mirror one another, and a mood can spread quickly. Yet this is contagion, not\nhypnosis: people remain aware of what they are doing.\n\nThis more cautious reading avoids both extremes. A crowd does not turn thinking people\ninto mindless machines, but it does not leave them wholly untouched either. Instead, it\ntilts behaviour rather than erasing the person, nudging people towards choices they\nmight have hesitated to make alone and in plain view.",
