      "title": "Inference & Multiple Choice — The Science of First Impressions",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "A first impression forms astonishingly fast. In a much-cited experiment carried out at\nPrinceton University in 2006, Janine Willis and Alexander Todorov showed volunteers\nphotographs of unfamiliar faces for just a tenth of a second — a hundred milliseconds —\nand asked them to rate qualities such as trustworthiness and competence. Remarkably, the\nsnap verdicts barely changed when other participants were given as long as they liked to\nstudy the same faces. The brain, it seems, reaches a conclusion almost before there is\ntime to think.\n\nWhat drives these instant judgments is not careful reasoning but a handful of crude cues.\nA face that resembles a typical 'trustworthy' expression, a confident posture or a warm\ntone of voice can tilt an assessment within seconds. The trouble is that such cues are\neasily misread. The qualities the brain is so quick to detect — honesty, intelligence,\ncompetence — are precisely the ones that a brief glance cannot reliably reveal.\n\nThis speed has real consequences. Studies of interviews, courtrooms and elections suggest\nthat the impression formed in the opening moments often colours everything that follows,\nan effect psychologists call the halo effect: once someone is judged attractive or\nlikeable, observers tend to assume other good qualities as well. A weak first impression,\nby the same logic, can be hard to undo.\n\nNone of this means first impressions are worthless. They evolved as fast, rough guides in\na world where hesitation could be dangerous, and they are sometimes accurate. The point is\nthat confidence and accuracy do not always travel together: people feel sure of their snap\njudgments long before there is any good reason to be. Whether such instant reactions can\never be fully overridden by slower, more careful thought remains a question psychologists\ncontinue to debate.",
