      "library": "l2_skill",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "**A**  For most of history, people whose eyesight failed simply had to manage as best they\ncould. The first true eyeglasses appeared in northern Italy around the 1280s, probably\nnear the city of Pisa. Before that, readers had used a 'reading stone' — a curved piece\nof glass laid on the page to make the letters look bigger.\n\n**B**  These early glasses used convex lenses, which curve outwards in the middle. Such\nlenses help people who are long-sighted: they can see distant objects clearly but\nstruggle to read small print up close. For ageing scholars who copied books by hand,\nthis was a life-changing invention.\n\n**C**  It took roughly two more centuries for the opposite problem to be solved. By the\nfifteenth century, makers had learned to grind concave lenses, which curve inwards.\nThese suited short-sighted people, who saw nearby things well but found distant objects\nblurred.\n\n**D**  The spread of glasses owed much to the printing press. After the 1440s, printed\nbooks became far cheaper, and many more people learned to read. As reading became\ncommon, the demand for spectacles grew quickly, and lens-makers became busy craftsmen\nacross Europe.\n\n**E**  One famous improvement came in the 1780s, when the American Benjamin Franklin grew\ntired of switching between two pairs of glasses. He joined two half-lenses, placing one\nfor distance above one for reading. These 'bifocals' let a single pair do two jobs at\nonce.",
