    {
      "id": "d52fa34b-35da-5f19-8984-074a17f50b46",
      "slug": "skimming-the-history-of-the-camera",
      "title": "Skimming — The History of the Camera",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "**A**  Long before anyone could keep a photograph, people already had a kind of camera. It was called the *camera obscura* — Latin for 'dark room'. In a darkened room or box, light passing through a tiny hole threw a moving picture of the world outside onto the opposite wall, often upside down. Artists used it to trace accurate drawings. Yet there was one large problem: the camera obscura could project an image, but it had no way to save it. The moment the light stopped, the picture vanished.\n\n**B**  The breakthrough came in 1839, when the Frenchman Louis Daguerre announced a process that finally fixed an image in place. His *daguerreotype* captured a scene on a silver-coated copper plate, producing a sharp picture that would not fade away. For the first time in history, a single moment could be frozen and kept forever. The pictures were beautiful, but each plate was costly and could not be copied, so photography stayed a luxury for the few.\n\n**C**  That changed near the end of the 1800s. Instead of heavy plates, inventors developed flexible rolls of film that could hold many shots at once. In 1900 the American company Kodak sold a simple box called the Brownie for about one dollar. Suddenly photographs were no longer just for the rich; ordinary families could afford to take pictures of holidays, weddings and children, and a whole hobby was born.\n\n**D**  The final shift arrived a century later. Digital cameras replaced film with an electronic sensor that turns light directly into data, so there is no film to buy or develop at all. Pictures can be viewed at once and deleted if they fail. Today most photographs are taken not on cameras but on phones, though where this rapid change will lead next remains uncertain.",
      "metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "Câu hỏi kiểu 'X NOT do được gì' → tìm động từ phủ định khả năng (could not / had no way to); ở đây 'had no way to save it' chính là điểm chốt đáp án.",
            "point": "Modal 'could' diễn tả khả năng trong quá khứ",
            "review": "could + V(nguyên mẫu) = khả năng/được phép trong quá khứ. Sau modal, động từ luôn ở dạng nguyên mẫu, không thêm -s, không chia thì.",
            "example": "the camera obscura **could project** an image, but it had no way to save it.",
            "analysis": "'could' (quá khứ của 'can') + động từ nguyên mẫu không 'to' (project) diễn tả khả năng trong quá khứ. Câu đối lập hai vế bằng 'but': làm được điều này (chiếu ảnh) nhưng không làm được điều kia (lưu ảnh)."
          },
          {
