      "body_markdown": "**A**  Long before modern clocks, people measured time using whatever they could see in\nthe sky and around them. Sundials used the shadow of an upright stick, which moved as the\nsun crossed the sky. But sundials only worked on sunny days, so the Egyptians and other\nancient peoples also built water clocks, measuring the hours by water that dripped from\none container into another.\n\n**B**  The first mechanical clocks appeared in Europe around the thirteenth century. These\nlarge metal machines were driven by hanging weights and turning gears, and they stood in\nchurch towers. They had no minute hand and often rang a bell to mark the hour. The tower\nbell was the only way many townspeople could know the time.\n\n**C**  A great step forward came in the seventeenth century with the pendulum. A pendulum\nis a weight swinging back and forth in steady beats. This even swing made clocks far more\naccurate than before, cutting the error from many minutes a day down to a few seconds. For\nthe first time, people could trust the time their clocks showed.\n\n**D**  As parts grew smaller, clocks did too. Coiled springs replaced hanging weights,\nwhich meant a clock no longer had to hang high or stand still. Slowly, makers produced\nclocks small enough to carry in a pocket. A pocket watch became a prized object, often\npassed from parent to child.\n\n**E**  The final step was to make clocks smaller still. In the early twentieth century,\nwristwatches — small enough to wear on the wrist — became common. Today most people carry\nexact time in their phones. Yet this long story all began with the same simple wish: to\ndivide a day into parts that everyone could share.",
