      "title": "Skimming — The Story of Coffee",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "**A** Each day the world drinks more than two billion cups of coffee. It is hard to believe that this everyday habit began with a plant growing wild on a few African hillsides, and that nobody knows for certain who first decided to drink it.\n\n**B** One popular story comes from Ethiopia. A young goat herder named Kaldi is said to have noticed that his goats became unusually lively after eating the bright red berries of a certain bush. Curious, he tried the berries himself and felt the same burst of energy. The tale may not be true, but it points to the right corner of the world: the coffee plant really did come from this region.\n\n**C** From Africa, the berries crossed the sea to Yemen, in the Arabian Peninsula. There, people learned to roast and brew the beans into a hot, dark drink. By the fifteenth century, the first coffee houses had opened in Arabian cities, where men gathered to talk, play games and share news over their cups.\n\n**D** Traders soon carried coffee further. It reached the cities of Europe, where coffee houses again became busy meeting places, and later it travelled to plantations in Asia and the Americas. In this way a wild berry from a few hillsides became one of the most traded goods on Earth.\n\n**E** Exactly how a bitter seed turned into a global ritual is still not fully clear, and perhaps the gaps in the story are part of its charm.",
