      "body_markdown": "The first public zoos to open their gates in nineteenth-century Europe were not designed to save anything. They existed to display exotic animals and entertain city crowds, who paid to gaze at lions, elephants and creatures shipped from distant colonies. Today, with more than 700 million people visiting zoos and aquariums each year, the institution survives — but the question of what it is actually for has grown sharper.\n\nDefenders point to conservation. Modern zoos run captive-breeding programmes for species that are vanishing in the wild, and a handful of dramatic rescues are often cited. Przewalski's horse, for instance, was bred in captivity after disappearing from the steppes and has since been reintroduced to grasslands it had vanished from. Zoos also fund field research and pay for the protection of habitats far from their own enclosures, work that rarely reaches the visiting public.\n\nCritics answer that such successes are rare and that the daily reality is cages, boredom and animals living far from their natural range. Yet even sceptical observers admit something the brochures get right: for a child who may never see a tiger or a gorilla otherwise, a day among living animals can spark a curiosity that documentaries rarely match. Wonder, the argument runs, is where care for the natural world often begins.\n\nSo the verdict is not simple, and it need not be. A zoo that exists chiefly to amuse, with conservation as an afterthought, looks harder to defend with every passing decade. But a zoo that puts research and the protection of wild populations ahead of mere display may still earn its place — provided it keeps proving, rather than merely claiming, that the animals behind the glass are part of something larger than a show.",
