    {
      "id": "ba4153fb-bed7-517a-982d-2fd22b217abc",
      "slug": "tfng-ynng-is-remote-learning-effective",
      "title": "True/False/Not Given & Yes/No/Not Given — Is Remote Learning Effective?",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "When schools and universities closed during the pandemic in 2020, education moved online\nalmost overnight. At the peak of the closures, UNESCO estimated that around 1.6 billion\nlearners in more than 190 countries were studying from home. The sudden experiment\noffered the largest test yet of a long-standing question: can remote learning match the\nclassroom, or is it only a poor substitute?\n\nThe evidence that has emerged since is mixed. Several large reviews found that, on\naverage, students learned less during periods of remote teaching than they would have in\nperson, with the youngest children and those from poorer households falling furthest\nbehind. Without a quiet room, a reliable connection or an adult nearby, many simply\ncould not keep up with the lessons being streamed to them. For these students, the\nmonths lost online could take far longer to make up.\n\nYet the picture is not uniformly bleak. For motivated, independent learners —\nparticularly older students and adults retraining for new careers — online courses can\nwork remarkably well, allowing them to study at their own pace and revisit difficult\nmaterial as often as they need. The flexibility that frustrates a distracted ten-year-old\ncan be a genuine advantage for a disciplined twenty-five-year-old.\n\nMost researchers now conclude that the format itself matters less than how it is used.\nWell-designed online teaching, with regular contact between teachers and students and\nprompt feedback, can rival a classroom; a stream of recorded lectures with no interaction\nrarely does. In this writer's view, remote learning is best seen not as a replacement for\nthe classroom but as a powerful tool that works only when it is thoughtfully designed.\nWhether schools will make the effort required to use it well, rather than simply\nswitching the cameras on, remains an open question.",
      "metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "Định vị danh từ trước 'that' rồi nhảy qua cả mệnh đề quan hệ để tìm động từ chính — bước bắt buộc với câu dài trong TFNG/Y-N-NG.",
            "point": "Mệnh đề quan hệ xác định với 'that'",
            "review": "Mệnh đề quan hệ xác định: N + that/which + V, không dấu phẩy, không thể bỏ N. Khi 'that' làm chủ ngữ thì không lược được.",
            "example": "The flexibility **that frustrates a distracted ten-year-old** can be a genuine advantage",
            "analysis": "'that' thay 'the flexibility' và làm chủ ngữ của 'frustrates', mở mệnh đề quan hệ xác định (không dấu phẩy) bổ nghĩa cho 'flexibility'. Chủ ngữ thật của động từ chính 'can be' vẫn là 'the flexibility'."
          },
