    {
      "id": "a2ebcdd1-a6dc-5747-9625-56c434acbfbc",
      "slug": "the-business-of-second-languages",
      "title": "The Business of Second Languages",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "About 43 percent of the world's people are thought to be **bilingual**, and many more\nare learning a new language right now. Far from being a **niche** hobby, language\nlearning has grown into a global business worth billions. Schools, employers and\nsmartphone apps all compete to teach the words that open doors abroad.\n\nSeveral forces drive this **demand**. As trade and travel cross borders, a shared\nlanguage smooths every deal, and English has become the world's main **lingua franca**.\nWorkers who can switch between languages are **prize**d by international firms. For\nmillions of families, **master**ing a second tongue is seen as an **investment** in\ntheir children's future. Governments, too, fund language teaching to boost trade and\ndiplomacy abroad: the European Union, for example, officially encourages every\ncitizen to learn two languages in addition to their mother tongue.\n\nThe way languages are taught has changed dramatically. Traditional classrooms and\ntextbooks now share the market with apps that turn study into a game, rewarding\nlearners with points and streaks. These platforms collect data on millions of users,\nallowing lessons to be **tailor**ed to each person. Some are free to download but earn\nmoney from advertising or paid **upgrade**s.\n\nYet technology has clear limits. Critics argue that apps are good at building\n**vocabulary** but weaker at teaching real conversation. Becoming truly **fluent** still\ntends to require months of practice with other speakers. Speaking aloud with a patient\nhuman still teaches rhythm and tone that screens often miss. Researchers also point out\nthat **motivation**, not the latest tool, is what most reliably **predict**s success.\n\nWhether apps can ever replace human teachers remains **doubtful**, and the most\neffective learners often combine several methods. What seems clear is that demand for\nsecond languages will keep rising as the world grows more connected. For the businesses\ninvolved, that promises a market unlikely to fall silent soon.",
