    {
      "id": "467499eb-dafc-5ee5-bd6c-e0231fc7279f",
      "slug": "the-economics-of-fast-fashion",
      "title": "The Economics of Fast Fashion",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "Between 2000 and 2014, the amount of clothing produced around the world roughly\ndoubled, while the average shopper bought about 60 percent more **garments** than\nbefore. Yet each item was kept for only half as long. This rapid cycle of buying and\n**discard**ing is the engine of an industry known as **fast fashion**.\n\nFast-fashion **retailer**s compete by turning the latest **catwalk** trends into\ncheap clothing within weeks. Low prices and constant new ranges encourage shoppers to\ntreat clothes as almost **disposable**. Because the **margin** on each item is thin,\nprofits depend on selling enormous volumes. The **supply chain** is often stretched\nacross many countries to keep labour costs down.\n\nThe hidden costs of this model fall heavily on the environment. Producing a single\ncotton shirt can consume thousands of litres of water, and **synthetic** fibres shed\n**microplastics** with every wash. The fashion industry is now thought to be\nresponsible for as much as a tenth of global **carbon emissions**, according to some UN\nestimates, although other studies put the true figure lower. Dyeing the fabric also\npollutes rivers in several producing countries, turning whole waterways unnatural\ncolours. Mountains of unsold or unwanted clothing are burned or sent to **landfill**.\n\nCritics argue that the true price of a cheap T-shirt is paid elsewhere — by underpaid\nworkers and polluted rivers. In response, some brands have begun to promote recycling\nschemes and more **durable** designs. Repair cafes and second-hand apps have also grown\npopular among younger shoppers. Governments in several countries are considering rules\nthat would make companies responsible for the waste they create.\n\nWhether such measures can slow the industry remains uncertain, since low prices are\nhard for many **consumer**s to resist. A genuine shift may depend less on guilt than\non clothes that are designed to last. For now, the real cost of fast fashion is still\nlargely hidden from view.",
      "metadata": {
