    {
      "id": "8d08718b-e798-5e2c-861c-abbba1452dd0",
      "slug": "the-economics-of-secondhand",
      "title": "The Economics of Secondhand",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "A generation ago, buying **secondhand** carried a quiet sense of embarrassment.\nToday, it is one of the fastest-growing parts of retail. From clothes and furniture\nto phones and cars, the market for used goods is enjoying a global **boom**: analysts forecast that the worldwide\nsecondhand clothing trade will roughly double to around 350 billion US dollars by 2028.\n\nThe most obvious **incentive** is money. Used items are simply more **affordable**\nthan new ones, and for many **consumers** that is reason enough. There is a second\nfinancial logic, too. Almost everything new begins to **depreciate** the moment it\nis bought, so a buyer who chooses a well-made used product avoids paying for that\nearly loss of value. Some shoppers even treat purchases as investments, choosing\nbrands with a strong **resale** market.\n\nTechnology has transformed the trade. Online **platforms** now let anyone photograph\nan unwanted jacket or sofa and sell it within minutes, reaching buyers across the\ncountry. Specialist **thrift** stores and charity shops have also become fashionable\ndestinations rather than places to hide.\n\nThere is a powerful environmental argument as well. Every reused item is one that\ndoes not need to be manufactured from scratch, and it stays out of **landfill** for\nlonger. For shoppers who want a more **sustainable** lifestyle, choosing used is an\neasy, practical step. Many also point out that older products can be surprisingly\n**durable**, built before companies designed goods to wear out quickly.\n\nPerhaps the biggest change, though, is cultural. The old **stigma** attached to\nsecondhand goods is fading fast, especially among younger buyers who see a good\nused find as something to celebrate rather than hide.\n\nWhether the boom can continue at its current pace is uncertain, and some worry that\nbig companies may simply turn reuse into another form of fast consumption. For now,\nhowever, the secondhand market shows how saving money and helping the planet can,\noccasionally, point in the very same direction.",
      "metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "Bị động giấu chủ thể → khi câu hỏi hỏi 'ai làm', kiểm tra xem 'by ai' có thực sự nêu không (bẫy NOT GIVEN).",
            "point": "Bị động hiện tại đơn + lược tác nhân (the moment it is bought)",
            "review": "Bị động hiện tại: S + is/are + V3. 'the moment + clause' = mệnh đề thời gian (= as soon as).",
            "example": "Almost everything new begins to depreciate the moment **it is bought**",
            "analysis": "'it is bought' là bị động hiện tại đơn (is + V3) trong mệnh đề thời gian rút gọn ('the moment' = ngay khi). Người mua được lược bỏ vì trọng tâm là sự việc 'được mua', không phải ai mua. 'begins to depreciate' là to-infinitive làm bổ ngữ sau 'begins'."
          },
