    {
      "id": "240747d2-8369-5101-a9d2-a027ea8083dd",
      "slug": "the-economics-of-streaming",
      "title": "The Economics of Streaming",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "Every time a song is played on a **streaming** service, a sum so small it is almost\ninvisible changes hands. Listeners pay a fixed monthly **subscription** rather than\npaying for each track, so many people assume that artists receive a set rate per play.\nThe reality, however, is far stranger.\n\nInstead of paying for each play separately, the platform gathers all the month's\nsubscription and advertising money into one large **pool**. Around two-thirds of that\n**revenue** is then handed to **rightsholders**, while the rest covers tax, card fees\nand the cost of running the service. The pool is **distribute**d by **market share**:\nan artist who accounts for one per cent of all streams receives one per cent of the\n**royalties**. Under this model, a play from a paying subscriber is worth more than\none supported only by advertising.\n\nThis **tiered** system, which many describe as largely **undisclosed**, leads to\nsurprisingly small figures. Each stream tends to be worth only a fraction of a cent,\nso a track must cross a minimum **threshold** of plays each year before it generates\nanything at all. Songs that fall below that line earn nothing, even if a few loyal\nfans replay them often. To earn a modest income, an **independent** artist may\ntherefore need hundreds of thousands of streams, and the exact amount also shifts with\nthe country in which the listening happens. In wealthier regions, each play tends to be\nworth a little more than the same play elsewhere.\n\nFor the biggest stars, who attract billions of plays, the model can still be highly\n**profitable**. For everyone else, it is hard to **sustain** a career on streaming\nroyalties alone, and many musicians now rely on concerts and merchandise instead.\nCritics argue that the formula rewards quantity rather than quality. Whether the model\ncan be\nadjusted to support artists more fairly remains uncertain — but for now, the economics\nof a single play stay surprisingly tiny.",
