      "id": "2484ceae-c04c-5eec-bf48-21b17b015dfb",
      "slug": "the-ethics-of-gene-editing",
      "title": "The Ethics of Gene Editing",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "In November 2018, the Chinese scientist He Jiankui announced that he had used the\ngene-editing tool CRISPR to **edit** the genomes of twin girls, intending to make them\nresistant to HIV. The claim caused alarm not because the technique itself was\nunprecedented; laboratories had in fact been refining its **precision** for years. It\ncaused alarm because the changes He had made were **heritable**. They affected the\n**germline**, the line of reproductive cells, and so would be carried by every one of\nthe twins' future descendants.\n\nThe scientific community moved almost as one to **condemn** the experiment. A year later\na court in Shenzhen sentenced He to three years in prison for the illegal practice of\nmedicine, after finding that he had forged ethical-review documents. There was, in\nshort, a broad **consensus** that the work had been premature, secretive and reckless.\nYet outrage at a single, badly handled case cannot, by itself, settle the deeper\nquestion of principle that the underlying technology continues to raise.\n\nAt the heart of the debate lies a fragile and much-disputed line. Most observers accept\n**therapeutic** edits intended to cure the cruel inherited diseases that blight families\nacross generations. Many, however, fear a gradual slide from cure towards\n**enhancement** — the deliberate selection of height, intelligence or appearance. That\nparticular fear is perhaps overstated, since few human **traits** depend on a single\ngene. Even so, the more sober arguments concede that the tool remains far from perfect,\nand that **off-target** cuts may still damage unrelated stretches of DNA.\n\nWhat makes germline editing especially fraught, however, is the fact that it is\n**irreversible**. Those who would be affected do not yet exist, and so can never give\ntheir **consent** to a decision taken on their behalf. Added to this is the persistent\nworry about **equity**, since an expensive therapy available only to the wealthy risks\nwidening the very inequalities that already divide societies.\n\nFaced with these dangers, some scientists have called for a temporary **moratorium**.\nOthers, doubting that any pause can really be policed, demand tighter and more permanent\noversight rather than an outright ban. A blanket **prohibition**, the second group warns,\nmight simply drive the work towards less careful jurisdictions where almost nothing is\nmonitored. The crux of the dilemma, in other words, is less a matter of technical\nfeasibility than one of self-imposed limitation — a determination of just how far a\nsociety is willing to go, and one it has yet to make.",
