      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "In 1840, a small piece of paper changed the world. It was the first **postage stamp**,\ncalled the Penny Black. Before this, sending a **letter** was slow and **costly**.\n\nLong ago, the person who got the letter paid for it. The price went up with the\n**distance**. Sending news was hard for poor families. Many people could not pay, so\nthey **refused** the letter. The system was a mess.\n\nA British man named Rowland Hill had a **clever** **idea**. He said the **sender**\nshould pay first. People could buy a cheap stamp and stick it on. Every letter would\ncost just one penny.\n\nThe first stamp was black, so people called it the Penny Black. It showed the face of\nQueen Victoria. The idea worked, and it spread around the world. Soon, other countries\ncopied it too. Today, this small **invention** still helps us **connect**.",
