    {
      "id": "fa50f34f-6101-581f-8dd2-c1020ce0e67c",
      "slug": "the-history-of-chess",
      "title": "The History of Chess",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "Long before computers could defeat grandmasters, a simple board game was already\ntravelling thousands of kilometres on the backs of camels and ships. Most historians\ntrace chess to a game called **chaturanga**, which appeared in India around the sixth\ncentury and was the **ancestor** of the game played today.\n\nChaturanga was, in a sense, a model of a **battlefield**. Its four kinds of pieces\nstood for parts of an army: foot soldiers, or **infantry**, horsemen, elephants and\nchariots. As the great Indian **empire** had contact with its neighbours, the game did\nnot stay still. It travelled along busy **trade routes** into Persia, where a\n**merchant** or a traveller could teach the rules to almost anyone he met.\n\nWherever the game arrived, players would **adapt** it. The names of the pieces changed,\nand so did their powers. By the time chess reached Europe, it had become a favourite\npastime of kings and the **nobility**. The aim, however, stayed the same: to trap the\n**opponent**'s king, which rewarded careful planning and clever **strategy**\nrather than luck.\n\nThe modern game finally took shape in the nineteenth century. Clubs across Europe began\nto **standardise** the rules, and in 1851 London hosted the first major international\n**tournament**. From a battlefield drawn on cloth to a sport played online by millions,\nchess may be one of the few games that has crossed so many borders and centuries while\nkeeping its heart unchanged.",
      "metadata": {
