      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "In October 1582, people in parts of Europe went to sleep on the 4th and woke up on the\n15th. Ten days had simply **vanish**ed from the calendar. This strange jump was not a\nmistake; it was a careful **repair** ordered by Pope Gregory XIII. He simply wanted the\ncalendar to match the seasons and the heavens once again.\n\nThe trouble began centuries earlier. In 46 BC, the Roman leader Julius Caesar set up a\ncalendar with 365 days and an extra day every fourth year. This rule was clever, but\nslightly too long. The real **solar** year is about eleven minutes shorter, so the\ncalendar slowly **drift**ed away from the seasons.\n\nBy the 1500s, the **gap** had grown to ten whole days. Important **religious** dates,\nsuch as Easter, would **slide** into the wrong season. To fix this, Gregory's experts\nremoved the extra days and **adjust**ed the leap-year rule. Years that end in 00 are now\n**leap year**s only if they **divide** neatly by 400.\n\nDifferent countries **accept**ed the new calendar at different times, sometimes\ncenturies apart. Today the Gregorian calendar is the most widely used system on earth.\nYet it is still not perfect, and tiny errors mean that one more small **correction** may\nbe needed in the distant future.",
