      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "In 1959, the linguist Roman Jakobson observed that languages differ less in what they can\nexpress than in what they oblige their speakers to express. That deceptively quiet remark\nsits at the heart of a long debate about whether some words are genuinely\n**untranslatable**.\n\nThe classic examples are single words dense with cultural meaning. Portuguese **saudade**,\noften glossed as a bittersweet longing for something absent, carries **connotations** that\nno English equivalent quite reproduces; the dictionary gloss **denotes** the idea but\nfails to **evoke** the feeling that native speakers attach to it. Linguists collect dozens\nof comparable cases, from Japanese words for the pathos of fleeting things to German names\nfor a wistful, undirected longing that points at no particular object.\n\nFor some theorists, such cases expose a deeper problem with the very notion of\n**equivalence**. If meaning is woven from a word's history, its **register**, and the web\nof associations it carries, then a translation can at best **approximate** the original,\ntrading perfect **fidelity** for readability.\n\nOthers regard this pessimism as overstated. Jakobson himself argued that whatever one\nlanguage leaves implicit, another can make explicit through **paraphrase**, description or\nborrowing; the translator who cannot find a single word can still **render** the sense\nacross a whole sentence, or simply import the term as a **calque**. By this account,\nnothing is strictly **ineffable**, merely inconvenient. The cost is not meaning lost but\nwords multiplied, since what one tongue compresses into a single syllable another may need\nan entire clause to unfold.\n\nThe practical art, then, lies in compromise. A **literal** rendering may preserve the\nsurface of the source text while sacrificing its **nuance**, whereas a freer version\ncaptures the feeling but quietly rewrites the **idiom**. Each choice privileges one kind\nof loyalty — to the author, or to the reader — over another.\n\nWhat remains contested is how much this really matters. If every act of reading already\nreinterprets a text, then translation may be only a more visible version of something we\nall do, and its supposed losses may be less a betrayal than the ordinary price of meaning\ncrossing a border — a price that, for now, no theory has shown how to avoid.",
