      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "When the psychologists Sheena Iyengar and Mark Lepper set up a tasting booth in a\nCalifornia supermarket, they expected the larger display to win. On some days\nshoppers met six varieties of jam, and on others they met twenty-four. The bigger\nselection did draw more curious passers-by, yet the outcome reversed at the moment\nof purchase: only about three per cent of those who saw the vast array bought a\njar, against roughly thirty per cent of those offered the modest one. That single\nstudy, published in 2000, became the most cited evidence for what is now called\nthe **paradox** of choice.\n\nThe claim that an **abundance** of options can make us worse off is deeply\n**counterintuitive**, since freedom to choose is usually treated as an unqualified\ngood. The writer Barry Schwartz, who popularised the idea, argued that excessive\noptions generate a kind of **overload** that ends in **paralysis**: confronted by\na wall of nearly identical products, many shoppers find the effort of comparison so\ndraining that they simply walk away, **deterred** rather than empowered by the\nsheer variety on offer.\n\nSchwartz further suggested that the damage continues after a decision is made, and\nhere he distinguished two psychological types. A **maximiser**, who insists on\nfinding the single best option, must weigh every possibility and is left dwelling\non the **foregone** alternatives, so that more choice tends to breed more\n**regret**. A **satisficer**, content with an option that is simply good enough,\nescapes much of that burden and reports greater satisfaction with the same outcome.\n\nSubsequent research, however, has complicated this tidy narrative. A large\n**meta-analysis** that pooled dozens of experiments concluded that the average\nchoice-overload effect was close to **negligible**, appearing reliably only under\nparticular conditions. Whether extra options overwhelm us seems to depend on\nfactors such as time pressure, expertise and how the alternatives are presented,\nall of which **moderate** the result.\n\nThe paradox of choice, then, has acquired considerable **nuance** since that first\njam display. More choice is neither the curse nor the blessing it has sometimes\nbeen made out to be; how much it helps or harms appears, on current evidence, to\nhinge on context and on the chooser, and the question is far from settled.",
