      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "The official length of a single second can now be stated with astonishing exactness, yet almost\nnobody can say what time itself actually is. That gap between measuring something and\nunderstanding it lies at the heart of one of the oldest puzzles in both science and philosophy.\n\nFor most of history, the units by which people divided their days were frankly **arbitrary**,\nfixed less by nature than by habit and convenience. The largest divisions, admittedly, did rest\non **celestial** facts that no culture anywhere could afford to ignore. A day corresponded to a\nsingle **rotation** of the Earth upon its axis, while a year matched one complete **orbit** of\nthe planet around the Sun. Calendars in almost every society were built upon these two great cycles.\n\nThe trouble was that the heavens themselves proved to be a slightly unreliable clock. The\nEarth's spin is gently **inconsistent**, slowing over long spans and wobbling in ways that\nearlier astronomers could not possibly detect. The natural day, as a result, is not quite the\nsteady ruler that it has always appeared to be. As physics advanced, researchers needed a far\nmore **precise** standard, one that would not drift with the turning of a single planet.\n\nThe eventual solution came from the smallest scale imaginable rather than the very largest.\nScientists discovered that certain particles vibrate at a fixed and dependable rate, and a clock\ncan simply count this **oscillation** instead of watching the sky. In 1967, accordingly, the\nworld chose to **redefine** the second using a single **atom** of the element caesium. One second became the\nduration of exactly 9,192,631,770 cycles of the radiation emitted during a particular\n**transition** inside that atom. The resulting standard is so stable that the best caesium\nclocks would not drift by more than a second over several million years.\n\nSuch impressive precision, however, says almost nothing about how the passage of time actually\nfeels to a human being. The experience of duration is deeply **subjective**, since an idle hour\ncan crawl while a joyful one seems to vanish in an instant. Our private **perception** of its passing, in other words, bears\nlittle relation to any steady atomic count. A few philosophers have gone further still,\nsuggesting that the very flow from past to future may be a kind of **illusion** woven by the mind. For all the accuracy of the modern clock, then, the deeper nature of time\nremains, as it has always been, a genuine **enigma**.",
