    {
      "id": "390b38d1-3827-5728-b846-cfc41323cab7",
      "slug": "the-psychology-of-nudges",
      "title": "The Psychology of Nudges",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "In 2008, the scholars Richard Thaler and Cass Sunstein popularised an idea of\nremarkable reach: that human behaviour can be steered not by command or by costly\npenalties but by gentle **nudge**s. A nudge, on their careful definition, alters\nbehaviour in a predictable direction without forbidding any of the existing options or\nsignificantly changing the economic incentives attached to them; it works, in other\nwords, through quiet persuasion rather than through outright **coercion**.\n\nThe central tool of this approach is what the authors call **choice architecture** —\nthe often invisible way in which options are arranged, ordered and presented to us.\nBecause no option is ever actually removed from the table, Thaler and Sunstein defend\ntheir method as a form of libertarian **paternalism**; yet that deliberately\nprovocative label hints, all on its own, at why the topic should prove so fiercely\ncontested.\n\nThe best-known instance is the power of the **default**. When citizens are enrolled\nautomatically into a pension scheme or an organ-donor register unless they actively\n**opt out**, participation typically soars, sometimes from a small minority to the\noverwhelming majority. The effect exploits both ordinary human **inertia** and a\ndeep-seated, predictable **bias** towards simply doing nothing; in much the same spirit,\nmaking the healthier choice more visible and **salient** quietly raises how often people\ntake it.\n\nYet it is the critics who subject the whole field to its sharpest **scrutiny**. If a\nnudge works below the level of our conscious awareness, they ask, does it not quietly\n**manipulate** us into decisions we never really chose? Defenders reply that a\n**transparent** nudge — one that people can readily see, understand and resist if they\nwish — leaves their **autonomy** essentially intact.\n\nThe evidence, too, demands a measure of humility, since several once-celebrated nudge\nstudies have lately proved stubbornly hard to **replicate**. No presentation, after\nall, is ever neutral; every possible arrangement of options steers us somewhere. So\nthe useful question is not whether people should be nudged at all, but something\nnarrower and harder: is the nudge open about its aims, well-intentioned in its design,\nand its architects **accountable** for what they build? On that question, the debate\nremains lively and, for now, genuinely unresolved.",
