      "body_markdown": "In almost every human society, roughly one person in ten is left-handed, a\n**proportion** that has stayed **remarkably** stable for thousands of years. Cave\npaintings and ancient tools suggest that the same rough balance existed in\n**prehistory**, and the pattern even appears, in a milder form, among many other animals.\nWhy most people **favour** the right hand, and why a steady **minority** does not,\nremains a genuine scientific puzzle.\n\nPart of the answer lies in the brain, since the two **hemisphere**s are not identical:\neach one controls the opposite side of the body and **specialise**s in different\ntasks. In most people the left hemisphere, which **govern**s the right hand, also\n**dominate**s language. **Handedness** therefore offers a visible clue to how an\nindividual brain is organised.\n\nThe causes of left-handedness are complex. Studies of twins show that genes play a\nrole, yet they are far from the whole story, since identical twins often differ in\ntheir preferred hand, and conditions in the **womb** and pure chance appear to matter\ntoo. No single gene 'for' handedness has ever been found.\n\nIf right-handedness is so common, why have left-handers not vanished? One popular idea\nis the 'fighting **hypothesis**'. Because most **opponent**s are used to facing\nright-handers, a left-hander may gain a surprise advantage in combat or sport, and\nstudies of fencers and baseball pitchers lend some support to this idea. Such an edge\ncould be enough to preserve the **trait**, even though left-handers face a world built\nfor the majority.\n\nNone of these explanations is complete on its own, and researchers still debate how\nthey fit together. What seems certain, however, is that left-handedness is not a\n**defect** but a natural **variation**, and the puzzle **endure**s partly because the\nhuman brain guards its secrets so closely.",
      "metadata": {
