      "title": "The Return of the Wolf",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "In the winter of 1995, biologists released 14 grey wolves into Yellowstone National\nPark, ending an absence of nearly seventy years. The animals had been hunted to local\n**extinction** in the 1920s, and the park had changed while they were gone. Their\nreturn became one of the most studied experiments in modern **ecology**.\n\nWithout a top **predator**, the park's elk had multiplied to around twenty thousand,\nand the large **herd**s **graze**d freely along the rivers, **strip**ping young\n**willow**s and aspen before the trees could **mature**. Riverbanks lost the roots\nthat once held the soil in place, while overgrazing also left bare ground that was\neasily washed away by spring floods. Songbirds and beavers, which depend on these\ntrees, became **scarce**.\n\nThe wolves set off a chain of effects that scientists call a **trophic cascade**. As\nthe predators thinned the herds and kept the elk on the move, **browsing** pressure\neased, and willows and aspen recovered while beavers returned to build their\n**dam**s. The new ponds created **habitat** for fish, insects and birds. Carcasses\nleft by the wolves fed eagles, ravens and bears throughout the long winter.\n\nSome changes reached even the rivers themselves: as **vegetation** returned, banks\ngrew more stable and a few streams began to wind less wildly. This reshaping of the\nlandscape by a single species is known as a **keystone** effect, and the wolf, it\nturned out, was woven tightly into the whole **ecosystem**.\n\nNot every scientist accepts the simplest version of this story. Some argue that\nclimate, drought and other predators also shaped the recovery, and that the\nwolf's role has been **overstate**d. What remains clear is that removing one\nspecies can unsettle an entire system — and that restoring it may bring surprises\nthat no one predicted.",
