      "id": "49483f74-9315-587a-96dc-333155d95e3c",
      "slug": "the-rise-of-board-games",
      "title": "The Rise of Board Games",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "Long before screens or electricity, people **gather**ed around boards to play. Some of the\noldest games ever known are over four thousand years old, and they show us how **ancient**\npeople relaxed.\n\nIn ancient Egypt, people played a game called Senet. Archaeologists **have found** Senet\nboards inside royal **tomb**s, including that of King Tutankhamun. Another game, the Royal\nGame of Ur, came from nearby Mesopotamia. In both games, players **race**d their pieces\nacross the board, trying to move faster than their **opponent**.\n\nBoard games **spread** around the world and changed over time. Some relied on luck,\n**while** others, such as chess, needed careful thought and **strategy**. In the\ntwentieth century, factories began to **mass-produce** cheap games. Games like Monopoly\nbecame **household** names in many countries.\n\nThen video games arrived, and board-game sales saw a clear **decline**. Yet board games\nnever vanished completely. Recent years have even shown a strong **revival**, with\nthousands of new titles released every year.\n\nWhy do such simple objects of paper and wood still attract people? One reason may be that\nthey bring people together face to face — something that screens do not always do, as\nfriends gather around a single table. After thousands of years, that simple pleasure of\nsitting down to play with others seems unlikely to fade away.",
