      "title": "The Rise of the Food Truck",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "In 2008, a small taco truck began driving around Los Angeles, selling Korean barbecue\nwrapped in a Mexican tortilla. Its young **chef**, Roy Choi, told fans where to find it\nusing short messages on Twitter. Crowds chased the truck across the city, and the\nmodern food truck was suddenly **fashionable**.\n\nThe idea was far from new. As early as 1866, a Texas **rancher** named Charles\nGoodnight rebuilt an old army **wagon** into a rolling kitchen. This \"chuck wagon\" fed\nhungry cowboys on long cattle drives. For more than a century afterwards, simple trucks\nsold cheap snacks to workers near factories and building sites.\n\nWhat changed was the food and the customer. Choi proved that a truck could serve fresh,\n**creative** dishes at a low price. Starting a truck costs far less than opening a\nrestaurant, so young chefs could test bold ideas without huge **debt**. Social media\nlet them reach **customer**s directly and move to wherever the **demand** was strongest.\n\nToday food trucks face new problems. City rules, parking fees, and **competition** from\ndelivery apps all squeeze their **profit**. Some owners use a popular truck as a first\nstep towards a **fixed** restaurant. The food truck may remain less a cheap meal than a\nclever way to launch a **brand**.",
      "metadata": {
