      "title": "The Science of Expertise",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "In the early 1990s, the psychologist Anders Ericsson and his colleagues studied\nviolin students at a celebrated music academy in Berlin, hoping to discover what\nseparates the merely competent from the truly outstanding. Their answer, published in\n1993, challenged a comfortable assumption. Where most observers credited the finest\nperformers with **innate** gifts, the researchers concluded that **expertise** owed\nfar more to the kind of training a person undertook than to any talent fixed at\nbirth.\n\nWhat **distinguished** the best students, the study found, was a particular form of\neffort the authors called **deliberate practice**: solitary, demanding and far from\nenjoyable, it is aimed squarely at correcting specific weaknesses rather than simply\nrepeating what one already does well. By the age of twenty, the leading group had on\naverage **accumulated** roughly ten thousand hours of such practice, considerably more\nthan their less accomplished peers, and it was this disciplined work, rather than\ncasual playing, that appeared to drive their superiority.\n\nThe research might have remained within psychology had a best-selling book not later\n**popularised** a single, memorable figure. In its retelling, the ten-thousand-hour\naverage hardened into a magic **threshold**, a fixed quantity of practice that anyone\ncould supposedly cross to reach the top in any field. Ericsson himself protested that\nthis neat slogan **oversimplified** his careful findings in two important ways. The\nnumber was only an **aggregate**, not a total that every gifted violinist had actually\nreached, and the round figure was in any case somewhat **arbitrary**, with no special\npower attached to it.\n\nMore seriously, the popular version mistook **quantity** of practice for the whole\nstory. Later analyses suggested that hours of practice explained only part of the\n**variance** in performance, and that the amount required differs sharply across\n**domains**, from chess to surgery to sport. The science of expertise, then, offers a\ngenuinely hopeful message, since skill can be built through the right kind of work\nrather than inherited. Yet the tidy promise of a magic number remains a\n**misconception**, and exactly how talent, training and circumstance combine to\nproduce mastery is, on present evidence, a question still very much open.",
      "metadata": {
