    {
      "id": "51ce2091-857b-5b68-99b8-5e8c946fe092",
      "slug": "the-science-of-forgetting",
      "title": "The Science of Forgetting",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "In 1885, a German psychologist named Hermann Ebbinghaus spent months trying to\n**memorise** long lists of meaningless **syllables** in order to study his own memory.\nHis results produced the famous **forgetting curve**, which shows how quickly new\ninformation **fades** from the mind. Within roughly the first hour, he found that\nabout 56 percent of freshly learned material had already slipped away. By the\nfollowing day, only a small **fraction** remained without any review.\n\nForgetting is not simply a **flaw** in the brain. Many researchers now argue that it\nis an active and useful process that helps people **discard** the **trivial** details\nof daily life. When a memory is first formed, it must be **encoded** and then\n**consolidated** before it can be stored. If a memory is rarely **retrieved**, the brain\ntends to treat it as unimportant, and the **trace** gradually weakens.\n\nSeveral factors influence whether a memory survives. **Interference** occurs when\nsimilar memories compete, so that newer information can overwrite older information.\nA familiar **cue**, such as a smell or a place, can suddenly trigger a memory that\nseemed lost. This explains why recall often depends on the situation in which\nlearning originally took place.\n\nModern studies suggest practical ways to slow the curve. **Spaced repetition**, in\nwhich material is reviewed at widening intervals, appears to strengthen memories far\nmore than **cramming**. Testing oneself, rather than simply rereading notes, also\nforces the brain to retrieve information actively. These techniques are now built\ninto many language-learning apps.\n\nThe science of forgetting remains incomplete, and the exact way the brain stores a\nsingle memory is still debated. What seems clear, however, is that forgetting and\nremembering are two sides of the same system. A mind that never forgot anything\nmight struggle to think clearly at all.",
      "metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "Văn học thuật ưa bị động để nhấn vào quá trình/đối tượng; khi gặp câu hỏi 'ai làm', kiểm tra xem 'by ai' có thật sự xuất hiện không (bẫy NOT GIVEN).",
            "point": "Câu bị động với động từ khuyết thiếu (modal passive) + lược bỏ tác nhân",
            "review": "Bị động + modal: S + modal + be + V3 (+ by O). Có thể lược 'be' lặp lại trong cấu trúc song song: must be encoded and (be) consolidated.",
            "example": "a memory is first formed, it **must be encoded** and then **consolidated** before it can be stored",
            "analysis": "'must be encoded' và '(must be) consolidated' là bị động đi với động từ khuyết thiếu: modal + be + V3. Tác nhân (não/người học) bị lược bỏ vì trọng tâm là bản thân ký ức, không phải ai thực hiện. 'consolidated' dùng chung 'must be' với động từ trước nhờ phép lược (ellipsis)."
