    {
      "id": "9d6e14d9-561a-5af8-a2e8-3c5f962ea0af",
      "slug": "the-science-of-glass",
      "title": "The Science of Glass",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "A common **myth** claims that the glass in very old windows is thicker at the bottom\nbecause it has slowly flowed downwards over centuries. In fact, glass does not flow at\nroom temperature; the uneven **pane**s simply reflect old methods of **manufacture**.\nYet the truth about glass is stranger than the myth.\n\nGlass is neither a normal solid nor a liquid. When most materials cool, their atoms\nlock into a neat, repeating pattern called a **crystal**. **Molten** glass, by\ncontrast, cools so quickly that its atoms freeze in a **disordered**, jumbled\narrangement. Scientists therefore describe glass as an **amorphous** solid.\n\nOrdinary window glass is made mainly from sand. About 73 percent of it is **silica**,\nthe **mineral** found in common sand, mixed with soda and lime to lower the\n**melting point**. Heating the same material and cooling it slowly would instead yield\na crystal. The ingredients are heated in a **furnace** to roughly 1,600 degrees Celsius\nuntil they **fuse** into a thick, glowing liquid. Tiny traces of other metals can be\nadded to give the glass colour. As this liquid cools, it becomes hard and\n**transparent** and never **crystallises**.\n\nProducing perfectly flat sheets was once difficult and expensive. In 1959 the British\nengineer Alastair Pilkington announced the **float process**, in which molten glass is\npoured onto a bath of liquid **tin**. Because the glass floats and spreads evenly, it\nsets with smooth, parallel surfaces. Almost all flat glass is still made this way\ntoday.\n\nGlass has another rare quality: it can be melted down and reshaped again and again\nwithout losing quality. This makes it one of the few materials that are, in theory,\nendlessly **recyclable**. Even so, much glass is still thrown away, and researchers\ncontinue to search for ways to make it lighter and stronger.",
      "metadata": {
