    {
      "id": "8211258b-3143-5cc5-9795-789ba77bf9bf",
      "slug": "the-secret-life-of-trees",
      "title": "The Secret Life of Trees",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "A forest looks so calm and **still**. But **beneath** the ground, something amazing is\nhappening. Trees do not live alone — they are **connect**ed in ways scientists are only\nstarting to understand.\n\nThe secret lies in the soil. Thin threads of **fungus** grow around the **root**s of trees\nand link them together. These threads form a hidden **network** that joins many trees. As\nit works a little like the internet, some people call it the \"wood wide web\".\n\nThe network does surprising things. Through it, big trees can **share** water and\n**nutrients** with **smaller** trees nearby. A healthy tree may even help a weaker one to\nsurvive. Trees can also send chemical **signal**s through the soil to **warn** neighbours\nof danger, such as when insects start to eat their leaves.\n\nThe Canadian scientist Suzanne Simard was one of the first to study these connections. Her\nwork showed that large, old trees can act as central points, sending help to many younger\ntrees around them.\n\nNot everyone agrees about how important this network is. Scientists still **debate** how\nmuch it really helps trees. Even so, one thing seems clear: a forest may be a far more\nconnected community than we once thought.",
      "metadata": {
