      "title": "The World of Coral Gardens",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "From space, the Great Barrier Reef looks like a chain of green and blue jewels\nstretching over 2,000 kilometres. Yet this giant structure is built by animals smaller\nthan your fingernail. Each tiny coral **polyp** is a soft, living **creature** with a\nring of arms.\n\nA polyp builds a hard cup of **limestone** around its body for protection. Millions of\nthese cups, **layer** upon layer, slowly form a coral reef. Inside each polyp live tiny\nplants called **algae**. The algae make food from sunlight and share it with their\n**host**, while the coral gives the algae a safe home. This close partnership is called\n**symbiosis**.\n\nCoral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor. Even so, they **shelter**\nabout a quarter of all sea **species**. Fish, turtles, and crabs feed and hide among\nthe colourful branches. For this reason scientists often call reefs the \"rainforests of\nthe sea\".\n\nWarm water now **threaten**s these gardens. When the sea heats up, stressed coral\npushes out its algae and turns ghostly white, an event known as **bleaching**. Without\nits partner, the coral may slowly **starve**. If oceans keep warming, many reefs could\nfade — though cooler, protected waters might still give them a chance.",
      "metadata": {
