    {
      "id": "61a779d2-2437-51f3-9919-76086699b950",
      "slug": "why-bees-matter",
      "title": "The Importance of Bees",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "Next time you eat an apple, you can thank a bee. Bees do much more than make honey.\nAs they fly from flower to flower, they **pollinate** plants — and without this, many\nof our **crops** would not grow.\n\nFarmers around the world **depend on** bees. Experts **estimate** that bees help\nproduce about one in three bites of the food we eat. In fact, the United Nations\nFood and Agriculture Organization (FAO) reports that pollinators such as bees\nplay a role in around 35 percent of the world's crop production. They give us\na wide **variety** of fruits, nuts, and vegetables, from almonds to strawberries.\n\nHow do bees do all this work? When a bee lands on a flower, it drinks the sweet\n**nectar** and carries tiny grains of pollen on its body. It then takes the nectar\nback to the **hive**, where the bees turn it into honey.\n\nSadly, bees are in trouble. Scientists have noticed a sharp **decline** in bee\nnumbers in recent years. There are several reasons. Some **pesticide** chemicals on\nfarms can harm bees. Disease is another **threat**. And as cities grow, wild bees\nlose their natural **habitat**.\n\nThe good news is that everyone can help. We can **protect** bees by planting more\nflowers in our gardens, buying local honey, and using fewer chemicals. Small actions,\ndone by many people, may be enough to keep these busy insects — and our food — safe.",
      "metadata": {
