    {
      "id": "26ea47aa-148c-5406-a994-f4ee5ecf838c",
      "slug": "why-we-dream",
      "title": "The Science of Dreaming",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "Every night, while you are **asleep**, your mind tells you strange stories. These\nstories are your **dreams**. People have always wanted to know one thing: why do we\ndream at all?\n\n**Scientists** still do not have one clear answer. But they have studied sleep for\nmany years, and they have some interesting ideas.\n\nWe dream most in one special **stage** of sleep. In this stage, the **brain** is very\n**active**, almost as busy as when we are awake. Our eyes also move quickly behind\nour closed eyelids.\n\nOne idea is about **memory**. While we sleep, the brain may **store** the important\nthings that happened during the day and forget the small ones. So dreams may help us\nremember.\n\nAnother idea is that dreams help us **practice**. In a dream, we sometimes run away\nfrom danger. This may train the brain for real life.\n\nMost people quickly forget their dreams. We often cannot **remember** them after we\nwake up, but the reason why we dream may still be a mystery.",
      "metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "'for many years' báo hiệu hiện tại hoàn thành (việc bắt đầu trong quá khứ, còn liên quan hiện tại).",
            "point": "Thì hiện tại hoàn thành (have + V3) chỉ kinh nghiệm/quá trình",
            "review": "Hiện tại hoàn thành: S + have/has + V3, đi với 'for + khoảng thời gian'. Phân biệt 'have studied' (V3) với 'have' (động từ chính = có).",
