    {
      "id": "41bfcea7-e794-523e-8eff-1797c1d8820f",
      "slug": "why-we-procrastinate",
      "title": "The Psychology of Procrastination",
      "library": "l1_vocab",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "The night before an important essay is due, a student suddenly decides that the\nkitchen must be cleaned, old photographs sorted, and several videos watched. The\n**deadline** is hours away, yet the work itself remains untouched. This familiar\npattern is what psychologists call procrastination, and a widely cited meta-analysis\nby Canadian psychologist Piers Steel found that about\n20 per cent of adults are chronic procrastinators. The reason people\n**procrastinate** is rarely the one most of us assume.\n\nFor a long time, delaying important work was blamed on simple **laziness**. Yet\nresearchers now describe procrastination as an emotional problem rather than a\nproblem of weak willpower. When a task feels boring, confusing or threatening, it\ncan **trigger** unpleasant feelings, and the mind looks for a way to escape them.\nProcrastination, in this view, is mainly an attempt to **regulate** emotion, not\nto avoid effort.\n\nThe trouble is that the relief does not last. Faced with **anxiety** about a\ndifficult task, the brain reaches for **avoidance**, and switching to something\neasier brings a sense of **temporary** comfort. Because the human brain tends to\nvalue an immediate **reward** more highly than a distant one, the **impulse** to\nfeel better now usually defeats the wish to finish later. The task remains, the\nstress returns, and the cycle repeats.\n\nUnderstanding this emotional root suggests better ways to **cope with** delay.\nOne useful **strategy** is to break a large job into small parts and begin with a\nsingle, manageable step, since starting is often the hardest moment. Surprisingly,\n**self-compassion** also helps: people who forgive themselves for past delays tend\nto procrastinate less, while harsh self-criticism only adds to the anxiety that\ncaused the problem.\n\nNone of this makes procrastination easy to defeat, and no single method works for\neveryone. Still, the research points to a hopeful idea: by treating the feeling\nrather than scolding the will, many people may protect both their calm and their\n**productivity**.",
      "metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "Bị động giấu chủ thể ('was blamed') — khi câu hỏi đòi 'ai đổ lỗi', kiểm tra xem 'by ai' có nêu rõ không (bẫy Not Given).",
            "point": "Câu bị động (passive voice) thì quá khứ, lược tác nhân",
            "review": "Bị động: S + be + V3 (+ by O). Lược 'by…' khi tác nhân chung chung. Danh động từ (V-ing) làm chủ ngữ số ít.",
            "example": "For a long time, delaying important work **was blamed on** simple laziness",
            "analysis": "Bị động quá khứ đơn 'was blamed' (was + V3), tác nhân (người ta nói chung) bị lược bỏ. Cụm động từ 'blame X on Y' chuyển sang bị động: X was blamed on Y. Chủ ngữ là cụm danh động từ 'delaying important work'."
          },
          {
            "tip": "'rather than' đánh dấu quan điểm tác giả khẳng định vế này, bác vế kia — chỗ hay ra câu hỏi về quan điểm/YNG.",
            "point": "Đối lập với 'rather than' (cấu trúc song song)",
            "review": "describe/see/regard X as Y. 'rather than + N/V-ing' = chứ không phải; hai vế phải song song hình thức.",
            "example": "researchers now describe procrastination as an emotional problem **rather than** a problem of weak willpower",
            "analysis": "'rather than + N' nối hai danh ngữ song song ('an emotional problem' ↔ 'a problem of weak willpower'), nêu sự phân biệt/đối lập. Đi với cấu trúc 'describe X as Y'."
