      "title": "Yes/No/Not Given & Inference — Is Economic Growth Sustainable?",
      "library": "l2_skill",
      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "For almost a century, the health of an economy has been judged largely by a single number: the rate at which the output it produces expands. Growth has become the central yardstick of a country's progress, a quantity said to be so bound up with employment, tax revenue and public wellbeing that questioning it sounds almost taboo. Yet an increasingly persistent question now shadows that consensus: whether ever-rising output is compatible with a finite planet.\n\nThe core worry is a physical one. Economic activity draws energy and raw materials from the natural world and discharges waste back into it, and no law guarantees that both flows can expand indefinitely without serious damage. Optimists reply that growth need not mean consuming more matter; an economy can grow by generating more value from fewer resources, a process economists call decoupling.\n\nOne version of this hope is captured in the so-called environmental Kuznets curve, the idea that pollution first rises with wealth but then falls once a society is rich enough to pay for cleaning up. The evidence for this pattern, however, is mixed at best: it seems to hold for some local pollutants but breaks down decisively for carbon emissions, which have continued to climb with incomes in most nations.\n\nFaced with such figures, some thinkers have advocated what they call degrowth — a deliberate, planned contraction of output in the wealthiest economies, paired with redistribution to protect the most vulnerable. The proposal meets fierce resistance, not only from vested interests but from those who fear that a shrinking economy would fall hardest on the very people it promises to help.\n\nIt would be comforting to conclude that technological ingenuity will certainly reconcile the two imperatives. Efficiency has indeed improved, and some nations have slowed the pace of damage per unit of output. But absolute decoupling — output rising while total real damage falls — remains elusive worldwide. The pressing question may therefore be less whether growth sounds reassuring than what kind of growth, if any, a finite planet can actually bear.",
