      "status": "published",
      "body_markdown": "In 1967 the philosopher Philippa Foot sketched a now-famous puzzle: a runaway trolley will kill five people unless it is diverted onto a track where it will kill one. For decades the scenario was a tool for testing intuitions in seminar rooms. Then engineers began building cars that steer themselves, and the puzzle acquired an uncomfortable new relevance, because a vehicle that must sometimes choose between harms is, in effect, a trolley problem rendered in steel and software.\n\nThe temptation to treat the two as equivalent should, however, be resisted. The classroom dilemma offers a tidy choice between two clearly counted outcomes, whereas a real vehicle confronts probabilities, partial information and split-second sensor readings; its clean, two-option form rarely matches the conditions on an actual road. Presenting the engineering challenge as a simple tally of lives flatters the analogy while disguising how much the messier reality leaves unresolved.\n\nWhat does carry over is the more troubling realisation that the choice cannot be left to the moment. A human driver who swerves acts on instinct and is judged afterwards with some allowance for panic. A programmed vehicle behaves according to a rule that someone, somewhere, has already written; the decision has been settled in advance, in an office, long before any emergency arises. That shift from improvised reaction to pre-authored policy is, arguably, the genuinely novel feature of the problem.\n\nIt also scatters responsibility in awkward ways. When an autonomous system causes harm, blame might fall on the engineers who set its priorities, the company that sold it, the owner who deployed it, or — less coherently — on the machine itself. None of these candidates fits the role comfortably, and the diffusion of accountability across so many hands is one reason the ethics of automation resists the neat resolutions the underlying mathematics can suggest.\n\nFor this reason the frequent proposal to remove the human from the loop altogether deserves scepticism. Doing so may reduce the erratic errors of tired or distracted drivers, yet it risks substituting a different danger: an outcome for which no identifiable person can readily be held answerable. Whether societies should hand such decisions to machines may therefore depend less on whether the machines can compute an answer than on whether anyone is prepared to stand behind the answers they give.",
