      "body_markdown": "In 2017, a much-discussed survey of 1,787 young American adults, published in the American Journal of Preventive Medicine, reported that those who spent the most time on social platforms also reported feeling the most isolated — a pairing that quickly hardened, in headlines at least, into a simple verdict: the apps were manufacturing a loneliness epidemic. The real picture, as is so often the case, resists such tidy summary.\n\nThe first difficulty is direction. Studies repeatedly find that heavier social-media use and higher loneliness tend to occur together, but correlation is not cause. It is entirely plausible that people who already feel lonely turn to their phones for company, rather than the phones producing the loneliness; the arrow could point either way, and untangling the two demands the kind of long-term data that has only recently begun to accumulate.\n\nA second complication is that the phrase \"using social media\" describes wildly different behaviours that researchers too often lump together. Passively scrolling through the carefully polished lives of others, comparing oneself unfavourably and saying nothing, looks rather different from actively messaging friends, organising a meet-up or keeping in touch with distant relatives one would otherwise rarely see. The former may well deepen a sense of isolation; the latter can sustain genuine friendships that the friction of distance would otherwise slowly erode.\n\nFor all these reasons, I would resist pinning rising loneliness on social media alone — it is too neat a story, and neat stories about complicated human problems are usually the ones worth distrusting. Loneliness has been climbing in many countries alongside changes in housing, longer and less stable working hours, the spread of single-person households, and the slow disappearance of the clubs, unions and places of worship that once stitched communities together. Social media is woven through this picture, certainly, and for some vulnerable users it may genuinely make matters worse; but to call it the single thread holding the whole thing up is to mistake a symptom for the entire disease — though I would not go so far as to say the platforms are blameless.",
      "metadata": {
        "grammar_focus": [
          {
            "tip": "Gạch ngang + cụm danh từ ở cuối câu = phần diễn giải/đánh giá của tác giả — hay chứa quan điểm để làm câu YNG.",
            "point": "Cụm đồng vị (appositive) sau dấu gạch ngang tóm ý cả mệnh đề",
            "review": "Cụm đồng vị (appositive) đặt sau gạch ngang/dấu phẩy để gọi tên hoặc tóm lại ý vừa nêu. report + V-ing = báo cáo rằng (đang)…",
            "example": "young adults who spent the most time on social platforms also reported feeling the most isolated — **a pairing that quickly hardened … into a simple verdict**",
            "analysis": "Sau dấu gạch ngang, 'a pairing that quickly hardened…into a simple verdict' là cụm danh từ đồng vị tóm lại nội dung cả mệnh đề trước (sự đồng xuất hiện), kèm mệnh đề quan hệ 'that quickly hardened…'. Tiếp đó dấu hai chấm dẫn cụm đồng vị thứ hai 'the apps were manufacturing a loneliness epidemic'. 'reported feeling' = report + V-ing."
          },
          {
            "tip": "'It is plausible/possible that…' là rào đón mạnh — tác giả nêu giả thuyết, KHÔNG khẳng định; câu YNG đòi khẳng định chắc thường là NOT GIVEN/NO.",
            "point": "Chủ ngữ giả + mệnh đề danh ngữ hoãn (extraposition) với tính từ rào đón 'plausible'",
            "review": "It is + adj (likely/plausible/clear) + that-clause = chủ ngữ giả, hoãn mệnh đề thật ra sau. rather than + V-ing = thay vì…",
            "example": "**It is entirely plausible that** people who already feel lonely turn to their phones for company, rather than the phones producing the loneliness",
            "analysis": "Chủ ngữ giả 'It' thay cho mệnh đề thật 'that…' được hoãn ra sau (extraposition); 'is entirely plausible' là tính từ rào đón. Khác it-cleft (it-cleft cần 'It be X that + chỗ trống'); đây chỉ là chủ ngữ giả + mệnh đề danh ngữ. 'rather than + V-ing' ('the phones producing…') là cấu trúc tương phản rút gọn. Đây là cách hedging: nêu khả năng thay vì khẳng định nhân quả."
          },
          {
            "tip": "'The former…the latter…' báo hiệu tác giả PHÂN BIỆT hai trường hợp → câu YNG nói 'tất cả… như nhau' thường là NO.",
            "point": "Đối lập song song 'The former … ; the latter …' (lược từ)",
